<subsection xml:id="subsec-vectorial-line-integrals">
  <title>Line Integrals of Vector Fields</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now integrate a vector field along a curve.  The key difference from
      scalar line integrals is that we take the <em>dot product</em> of the
      field with the tangent vector of the curve: only the component of the
      field that is <em>aligned with the motion</em> contributes.  The most
      important physical instance is <em>work</em>: the work done by a force
      field on a body moving along a path equals the vectorial line integral
      of the force along the path.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Definition: vectorial line integral ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-vectorial-line-integral">
    <title>Line Integral of a Vector Field</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>D \subseteq \R^n</m>, let
        <m>\mathbf{x}\colon [a,b] \to D</m> be a continuously differentiable
        curve, and let <m>\mathbf{v}\colon D \to \R^n</m> be a continuous
        vector field.  The <term>line integral of the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{v}</m> along <m>\mathbf{x}</m></term> is
        <me>
          \int_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{x}
          := \int_a^b
             \mathbf{v}\!\left(\mathbf{x}(t)\right) \cdot \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)
             \, dt,
        </me>
        where the dot denotes the Euclidean inner product and
        <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = \mathbf{x}'(t)</m> is the tangent vector.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ─── Remark: work interpretation ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-work-force-field">
    <title>Physical Interpretation: Work Done by a Force Field</title>
    <p>
      If <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is a force field and <m>\mathbf{s}\colon [a,b] \to \R^n</m>
      is the path of a moving body, the <term>work</term> done by the force
      on the body is
      <me>
        W = \int_{\mathbf{s}} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{s}
          = \int_a^b \mathbf{F}\!\bigl(\mathbf{s}(t)\bigr)
            \cdot \dot{\mathbf{s}}(t) \, dt.
      </me>
      We can rewrite this by separating the speed and the unit tangent:
      <me>
        W = \int_a^b
            \left[
              \mathbf{F}\!\bigl(\mathbf{s}(t)\bigr)
              \cdot \frac{\dot{\mathbf{s}}(t)}{\|\dot{\mathbf{s}}(t)\|}
            \right]
            \|\dot{\mathbf{s}}(t)\| \, dt.
      </me>
      The bracketed factor is the <em>orthogonal projection</em> of
      <m>\mathbf{F}</m> onto the unit tangent: it is the component of the
      force acting along the direction of motion.  Only this tangential
      component does work; the normal component is perpendicular to the
      displacement and contributes nothing.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-work-path">
      <caption>
        A path <m>\mathbf{s}</m> through a force field <m>\mathbf{F}</m>.
        At each point the tangential component (blue projection) of
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m> contributes to the work; the normal component
        (dashed) does not.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-work-path" width="55%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
            % Draw a smooth path s
            \draw[blue!70!black, very thick, ->]
              plot[smooth, tension=0.7]
              coordinates {(0.2,0.4) (1.0,0.9) (2.0,1.1) (3.0,0.8) (3.8,0.5)};
            \node[blue!70!black, below, font=\small] at (2.0,0.65) {$\mathbf{s}$};
            % A point on the path (roughly at t=0.5)
            \coordinate (P) at (2.0,1.1);
            \filldraw[black] (P) circle (1.5pt);
            % Tangent vector (unit tangent approximated)
            \coordinate (T) at (2.7,1.05); % direction (0.7,-0.05) normalized
            \draw[->,very thick,black] (P) -- (T)
              node[right,font=\small] {$\dot{\mathbf{s}}$};
            % Force vector F (some direction)
            \coordinate (F) at (2.6,1.9);
            \draw[->,thick,red!70!black] (P) -- (F)
              node[above right,font=\small] {$\mathbf{F}$};
            % Projection of F onto tangent direction
            % tangent unit direction approx (0.997,-0.071)
            % F vector: (0.6, 0.8), projection = 0.6*0.997+0.8*(-0.071)=0.598-0.057=0.541
            % projected point: P + 0.541*(0.997,-0.071) = (2.0+0.54, 1.1-0.038) = (2.54,1.06)
            \coordinate (Fproj) at (2.54,1.06);
            \draw[->,thick,blue!50!black] (P) -- (Fproj)
              node[below,font=\small] {proj};
            % Dashed normal component
            \draw[dashed,gray] (Fproj) -- (F);
            % Small arc for angle
            \draw[gray] ($(P)+(0.25,0)$) arc (0:52:0.25)
              node[midway,right,font=\scriptsize,gray] {$\theta$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Remark: computation procedure ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-vectorial-computation">
    <title>How to Compute a Vectorial Line Integral</title>
    <p>
      To evaluate
      <m>\displaystyle\int_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{x}</m>,
      follow three steps.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 1: Parametrize the curve.</em>
      Find a continuously differentiable parametrization
      <m>\mathbf{x}\colon [a,b] \to \R^n</m> whose trace is the desired path.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 2: Compute the tangent vector.</em>
      Differentiate: <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = \mathbf{x}'(t)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3: Form the dot product and integrate.</em>
      Evaluate the ordinary integral
      <me>
        \int_a^b
        \mathbf{v}\!\left(\mathbf{x}(t)\right) \cdot \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)
        \, dt.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Note that the vectorial line integral is <em>orientation-sensitive</em>:
      reversing the direction of traversal changes the sign of the result,
      unlike the scalar line integral.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Example: quarter circle ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-quarter-circle-vli">
    <title>Vectorial Line Integral Along a Quarter Circle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the line integral of the vector field
        <m>\mathbf{v}\colon \R^2 \to \R^2</m>,
        <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} x^2 \\ -xy \end{pmatrix}</m>,
        along the quarter circle in the first quadrant, traversed from
        <m>(1,0)</m> to <m>(0,1)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Step 1: Parametrize.</em>
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}\colon \left[0,\tfrac{\pi}{2}\right] \to \R^2, \qquad
          t \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} \cos t \\ \sin t \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Step 2: Tangent vector.</em>
        <me>
          \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)
          = \begin{pmatrix} -\sin t \\ \cos t \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Step 3: Compute the dot product.</em>
        Since <m>x = \cos t</m>, <m>y = \sin t</m>:
        <me>
          \mathbf{v}\!\left(\mathbf{x}(t)\right)
          = \begin{pmatrix} \cos^2 t \\ -\cos t \sin t \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
        Therefore
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{v}\!\left(\mathbf{x}(t)\right) \cdot \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)
            \amp= \cos^2 t \cdot (-\sin t)
               + (-\cos t \sin t) \cdot \cos t
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= -\sin t \cos^2 t - \sin t \cos^2 t
               = -2\sin t \cos^2 t.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 4: Integrate.</em>
      Using the substitution <m>u = \cos t</m>,
      <m>du = -\sin t \, dt</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{x}
            \amp= \int_0^{\pi/2} (-2\sin t \cos^2 t)\, dt
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \int_1^0 2u^2 \, du
               = -\int_0^1 2u^2 \, du
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= -2 \left[\frac{u^3}{3}\right]_0^1
               = -\frac{2}{3}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Reversing the orientation of the same quarter circle would change the
        sign of the integral to <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Theorem: fundamental theorem for line integrals ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-fundamental-theorem-line-integrals">
    <title>Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>D \subseteq \R^n</m>, let
        <m>\mathbf{v}\colon D \to \R^n</m> be a vector field, and
        let <m>f\colon D \to \R</m> be a differentiable antiderivative of
        <m>\mathbf{v}</m> (i.e.\ <m>\nabla f = \mathbf{v}</m>).  Then for every
        continuously differentiable curve <m>\mathbf{x}\colon [a,b] \to D</m>:
        <me>
          \int_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{x}
          = f\!\left(\mathbf{x}(b)\right) - f\!\left(\mathbf{x}(a)\right).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Two important consequences follow immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Closed curves give zero.</em>
        If <m>\mathbf{x}(a) = \mathbf{x}(b)</m> (i.e.\ the curve is closed),
        then
        <me>
          \int_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{x} = 0.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Path independence.</em>
        The integral depends only on the start and end points, not on the
        path taken between them: if <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}</m>
        are two curves in <m>D</m> with the same start and end points, then
        <me>
          \int_{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{x}
          = \int_{\mathbf{y}} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{y}.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <!-- ADDED: proof to justify the boundary-evaluation formula via the chain rule -->
    <proof>
      <p>
        Consider the composite function
        <m>g(t) = f(\mathbf{x}(t))</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>.  By the chain rule,
        <me>
          g'(t)
          = \nabla f\!\left(\mathbf{x}(t)\right) \cdot \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)
          = \mathbf{v}\!\left(\mathbf{x}(t)\right) \cdot \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t).
        </me>
        Integrating from <m>a</m> to <m>b</m> and applying the ordinary
        fundamental theorem of calculus gives
        <me>
          \int_a^b \mathbf{v}\!\left(\mathbf{x}(t)\right)
          \cdot \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)\,dt
          = \int_a^b g'(t)\,dt
          = g(b)-g(a)
          = f\!\left(\mathbf{x}(b)\right)-f\!\left(\mathbf{x}(a)\right).
        </me>
        This is exactly the stated formula.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <!-- Figure: path independence illustration -->
  <figure xml:id="fig-path-independence">
    <caption>
      Left: two different paths <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\mathbf{y}</m>
      between the same endpoints <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> give the same
      integral value for a conservative field.
      Right: a closed curve in a conservative field always gives integral
      value zero.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-path-independence" width="65%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
          % Left panel: two paths from A to B
          \begin{scope}
            \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
            \coordinate (B) at (3.2,0);
            \filldraw[black] (A) circle (1.5pt) node[left,font=\small] {$A$};
            \filldraw[black] (B) circle (1.5pt) node[right,font=\small] {$B$};
            % upper path
            \draw[blue!70!black, thick, ->, bend left=45] (A) to
              node[above,font=\small] {$\mathbf{x}$} (B);
            % lower path
            \draw[red!60!black, thick, ->, bend right=40] (A) to
              node[below,font=\small] {$\mathbf{y}$} (B);
            % equality annotation
            \node[font=\small] at (1.6,0) {$=$};
          \end{scope}
          % Right panel: closed curve
          \begin{scope}[xshift=4.8cm]
            \coordinate (O) at (1.2,0);
            \filldraw[black] (O) circle (1.5pt) node[below right,font=\small] {$A=B$};
            \draw[green!50!black, thick, ->]
              (O) to[out=60,in=10] (0.5,1.3) to[out=190,in=130] (-0.3,0.5)
              to[out=-50,in=200] (O);
            \node[green!50!black, above, font=\small] at (0.6,1.4) {$\oint = 0$};
          \end{scope}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <!-- ─── Remark: when does an antiderivative exist? ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-antiderivative-existence">
    <title>Existence and Computation of Antiderivatives</title>
    <p>
      The fundamental theorem raises three natural questions.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>1. Does every continuous vector field have an antiderivative?</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      No.  A simple counterexample is the rotation field
      <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} -y \\ x \end{pmatrix}</m>.
      Suppose <m>f</m> were an antiderivative.  Then
      <m>\partial f/\partial x = -y</m>, so integrating in <m>x</m>:
      <m>f(x,y) = -yx + g(y)</m>.
      Differentiating in <m>y</m>:
      <m>\partial f/\partial y = -x + g'(y) \overset{!}{=} x</m>,
      which forces <m>g'(y) = 2x</m>.  But <m>g</m> depends only on
      <m>y</m>, so this is a contradiction.  The rotation field is
      <em>not</em> conservative.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>2. When does an antiderivative exist?</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      A necessary condition (in <m>\R^3</m>) is that the curl vanishes:
      <me>
        \mathbf{v} \text{ has an antiderivative}
        \implies
        \operatorname{rot}\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}.
      </me>
      This follows from Schwarz's theorem on the equality of mixed partial
      derivatives.  For a continuously differentiable vector field on a simply
      connected domain, the converse also holds:
      <m>\operatorname{rot}\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}</m> implies
      <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is conservative.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the planar case <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = (P(x,y),Q(x,y))</m>, the
      corresponding condition is
      <m>\partial Q/\partial x = \partial P/\partial y</m>.
      This is the two-dimensional version students will use most often in
      examples.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>3. How to find an antiderivative, if one exists?</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      Use successive integration: integrate the first component equation in
      <m>x_1</m>, then determine the unknown function of the remaining
      variables from the second component equation, and so on.
      The following example illustrates the method in three variables.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let
      <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y,z)
         = \begin{pmatrix} e^x y + 1 \\ e^x + z \\ y \end{pmatrix}</m>.
      We seek <m>f</m> with <m>\nabla f = \mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Integrate <m>\partial f/\partial x = e^x y + 1</m> in <m>x</m>:</em>
      <me>
        f(x,y,z) = e^x y + x + g(y,z).
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Use <m>\partial f/\partial y = e^x + z</m>:</em>
      <me>
        e^x + \frac{\partial g}{\partial y}(y,z) = e^x + z
        \implies
        \frac{\partial g}{\partial y} = z
        \implies
        g(y,z) = zy + h(z).
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Use <m>\partial f/\partial z = y</m>:</em>
      <me>
        y + h'(z) = y
        \implies
        h'(z) = 0
        \implies
        h(z) = C \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore
      <me>
        f(x,y,z) = e^x y + x + zy + C, \qquad C \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Example: Exercise 4 — conservative field, two paths ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-conservative-path-independence">
    <title>Work Along Two Paths in a Conservative Field</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The force field <m>\mathbf{v}\colon \R^2 \to \R^2</m> is defined by
        <me>
          \mathbf{v}(x,y)
          = \begin{pmatrix} x^2 + y \\ x + \cos y \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
        The curve <m>\mathbf{s}_1</m> runs along the <m>x</m>-axis from
        <m>(1,0)</m> to <m>(-1,0)</m>, and <m>\mathbf{s}_2</m> connects the
        same two points along the upper unit semicircle.
      </p>
      <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
          <p>
            Compute the work done along <m>\mathbf{s}_1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Compute the work done along <m>\mathbf{s}_2</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find an antiderivative <m>F\colon \R^2 \to \R</m> of
            <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <figure xml:id="fig-two-paths">
        <caption>
          The straight path <m>\mathbf{s}_1</m> (on the <m>x</m>-axis) and
          the semicircular path <m>\mathbf{s}_2</m> share the same endpoints
          <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(-1,0)</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-two-paths" width="50%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.4]
              \draw[->,gray!60] (-1.6,0) -- (1.6,0) node[right,font=\small] {$x$};
              \draw[->,gray!60] (0,-0.3) -- (0,1.5) node[above,font=\small] {$y$};
              % s1: straight line on x-axis
              \draw[blue!70!black, very thick, ->]
                (1,0) -- (-1,0);
              \node[blue!70!black, below, font=\small] at (0,-0.12) {$\mathbf{s}_1$};
              % s2: upper semicircle
              \draw[red!60!black, very thick, ->]
                (1,0) arc (0:90:1) coordinate (M);
              \draw[red!60!black, very thick]
                (0,1) arc (90:180:1);
              \node[red!60!black, above right, font=\small] at (M) {$\mathbf{s}_2$};
              % endpoints
              \filldraw[black] (1,0) circle (1.2pt)
                node[below right,font=\small] {$(1,0)$};
              \filldraw[black] (-1,0) circle (1.2pt)
                node[below left,font=\small] {$(-1,0)$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Part (a): Work along <m>\mathbf{s}_1</m>.</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        Parametrize the straight path from <m>(1,0)</m> to <m>(-1,0)</m>:
        <me>
          \mathbf{s}_1\colon [-1,1] \to \R^2, \qquad
          t \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} -t \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
        (At <m>t=-1</m> we are at <m>(1,0)</m>; at <m>t=1</m> at
        <m>(-1,0)</m>.)
        The tangent vector is
        <m>\dot{\mathbf{s}}_1(t) = \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>.
        The field along the path:
        <me>
          \mathbf{v}\!\left(\mathbf{s}_1(t)\right)
          = \begin{pmatrix} (-t)^2 + 0 \\ -t + \cos 0 \end{pmatrix}
          = \begin{pmatrix} t^2 \\ -t + 1 \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
        Therefore
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_{\mathbf{s}_1} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{s}_1
            \amp= \int_{-1}^1
                 \begin{pmatrix} t^2 \\ -t+1 \end{pmatrix}
                 \cdot
                 \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
                 \, dt
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \int_{-1}^1 (-t^2) \, dt
               = \left[-\frac{t^3}{3}\right]_{-1}^1
               = -\frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{3}
               = -\frac{2}{3}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Part (c): Antiderivative of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.</em>
        (We solve (c) before (b) since (b) uses the result.)
      </p>
      <p>
        We search for <m>F</m> with <m>\nabla F = \mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Integrate <m>\partial F/\partial x = x^2 + y</m>:
        <me>
          F(x,y) = \frac{x^3}{3} + xy + C(y).
        </me>
        Use <m>\partial F/\partial y = x + \cos y</m>:
        <me>
          x + C'(y) = x + \cos y
          \implies
          C'(y) = \cos y
          \implies
          C(y) = \sin y + C_0, \quad C_0 \in \R.
        </me>
        Therefore
        <me>
          F(x,y) = \frac{x^3}{3} + xy + \sin y + C_0.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Part (b): Work along <m>\mathbf{s}_2</m>.</em>
      </p>
      <p>
        <!-- ADDED: make the role of the theorem explicit before using it on the semicircle -->
        Since <m>F</m> is an antiderivative, <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is conservative
        and the integral depends only on the endpoints, not on the particular
        path.  So we do not need a separate parametrization of the semicircle.
        By the fundamental theorem:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int_{\mathbf{s}_2} \mathbf{v} \cdot d\mathbf{s}_2
            \amp= F(-1,0) - F(1,0)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= \left(\frac{(-1)^3}{3} + (-1)(0) + \sin 0 + C_0\right)
               - \left(\frac{1^3}{3} + 1 \cdot 0 + \sin 0 + C_0\right)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= -\frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{3}
               = -\frac{2}{3}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        As expected, both paths give the same work value
        <m>-\tfrac{2}{3}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-vectorial-line-integrals-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-vectorial-line-integral-conservative-concept">
      <title>Path Independence</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If a vector field is conservative, what determines the value of the line integral between two points?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Only the endpoints</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. In a conservative field, the integral is path independent and equals a boundary evaluation of a potential.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Only the length of the path</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Different paths of the same length can still have different integrals in nonconservative fields.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Only whether the path is closed</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The endpoints matter; closed curves are only a special case.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Only the maximum value of the field along the path</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. That does not determine a line integral.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-vectorial-line-integral-constant-field">
      <title>Integrate a Constant Field Along a Segment</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute the line integral of the constant vector field
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}=\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\end{pmatrix}
          </me>
          along the line segment from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(1,3)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A potential for this field is
          <me>
            F(x,y)=x+2y.
          </me>
          Therefore, by the fundamental theorem for line integrals,
          <me>
            \int_C \mathbf{v}\cdot d\mathbf{s}
            = F(1,3)-F(0,0)
            = (1+2\cdot 3)-0
            = 7.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
